Upgrading Dibi and Texy
***********************

{{description: Upgrade Dibi and Texy automatically: dresscode/upgrade-deegee carries the migrations of Dibi from 4.1 and Texy from 3.0, which DressCode rewrites or reports.}}

.[perex]
The package `dresscode/upgrade-deegee` carries the migrations of the Dibi database layer from version 4.1 and of Texy from version 3.0: renamed classes and constants, methods moved to `Texy\Helpers`, renamed parameters, and API that disappeared without a replacement.


Installation
============

```shell
composer require --dev dresscode/upgrade-deegee phpstan/phpstan
vendor/bin/dresscode upgrade
```

That is all an upgrade needs: `upgrade` takes the data of the package by itself, with no configuration, and leaves the style of the code as it is. [Upgrading Code |upgrade] describes the procedure and what to do with what is left.

If you want every check to catch the old API too, put the types and the set `deprecations` into the configuration, as described in [How Upgrading Libraries Works |upgrading-libraries#Enabling It in the Configuration]:

```neon
typeAnalysis: phpstan

use:
	- perCs           # your standard
	- deprecations
```


What Gets Fixed
===============

A repository written for Dibi 4 and Texy 3.1, in a project on the current versions:

```php .[before]
public function insert(array $data): int
{
	$this->db->query('INSERT INTO articles', $data);
	return $this->db->insertId();  // Method `Dibi\Connection::insertId()` is replaced by `Connection::getInsertId()`.
}


public function render(string $text): string
{
	$html = $this->texy->process($text);
	return $this->texy->unProtect($html);  // Method `Texy\Texy::unProtect()` is replaced by `Texy::unprotect()`.
}


public function getColumnType(): string
{
	return Type::DATETIME;  // Constant `Dibi\Type::DATETIME` is replaced by `Type::DateTime`.
}
```

After `dresscode upgrade`:

```php .[after]
public function insert(array $data): int
{
	$this->db->query('INSERT INTO articles', $data);
	return $this->db->getInsertId();
}


public function render(string $text): string
{
	$html = $this->texy->process($text);
	return $this->texy->unprotect($html);
}


public function getColumnType(): string
{
	return Type::DateTime;
}
```

From Dibi, these get fixed: the constants of `Dibi\Type`, `Fluent` and the drivers renamed from uppercase, the constants of the static facade `dibi` (`dibi::AFFECTED_ROWS` to `Dibi\Fluent::AffectedRows`), `affectedRows()` and `insertId()` to `getAffectedRows()` and `getInsertId()`, also on the facade, `Dibi\DateTime::modifyClone($modify)` to `modify($modify)`, `DibiExtension22` to `DibiExtension3`, and the constructors of `FileLogger` and `Panel` with the filter `null`. From Texy: classes with the prefix `Texy` to the namespace `Texy\`, helper methods of `Texy` and `Utf` moved to `Texy\Helpers`, `escapeHtml()` to `htmlspecialchars()`, `unescapeHtml()` to `Texy\Helpers::unescapeHtml()` (and, in a project on Texy 3.0 to 3.1.0, to `html_entity_decode()`), the property `$cleaner` to `$htmlOutputModule`, the constants of `HtmlElement`, and the renamed parameters of `Modifier`.


What Is Left to You
===================

Where there is no replacement, DressCode reports the place and says what to do next. For example, the method `translateArgs()`, which Dibi 4.2 removed, or output modes Texy no longer has:

```
Method `Dibi\Connection::translateArgs()` is forbidden: there is no replacement; the hook is gone; the SQL is generated by `translate()`, which is final and takes the arguments as variadic parameters.
Method `Texy\Texy::setOutputMode()` is forbidden: there is no replacement; the output is always HTML5.
```

From Dibi, DressCode also reports the extension methods `extensionMethod()`, the trait `Dibi\Strict`, `modifyClone()` without an argument, and `__sleep()` and `__wakeup()` of a child of `Connection`, which should be replaced by `__serialize()` and `__unserialize()`. From Texy: the HTML4 and XHTML output modes, including `setOutputMode()`, `getOutputMode()` and `$xhtml`, encodings other than UTF-8 (`$encoding`, `Utf::toUtf()`, `utfTo()` and `utf2html()`), methods of modules that became private in version 3.2, `TexyMacro` for Latte 2, `$removeOptional`, `Modifier::$cite` and the trait `Texy\Strict`.

When a library only quietly marks something as deprecated, it is not in the data; with types, DressCode reports it, and where the annotation names the replacement, it also rewrites it through `upgrading.declarations.deprecatedMember`. The data does not capture changes in behavior at all.


What the Package Covers
=======================

| library | versions |
|---|---|
| `dibi/dibi` | 4.1 to 5.1 |
| `texy/texy` | 3.0 to 3.2 |

[Upgrading Code |upgrade#One Major Version at a Time] describes how to proceed one major version at a time.
